<Layout title="The Case for Caution: Long-Term NMDA Antagonism, Ketamine, and Depression">
    <header>
      <h1>The Case for Caution: Long-Term NMDA Antagonism, Ketamine, and Depression</h1>
      <div class="metadata">
        <time datetime="2026-09-28">Sep 28, 2026</time>
        <span class="separator" aria-hidden="true">•</span>
        <span class="author">Shasta Fox</span>
      </div>
    </header><blockquote><strong>Research essay.</strong> Author-submitted narrative interpretation. Full reference verification and independent peer review remain pending. Evidence from frequent nonmedical ketamine use is distinct from evidence on supervised treatment; associations do not establish causation.</blockquote>

    <div>
      <section>
        <p class="lead-paragraph">Ketamine can rapidly reduce depressive symptoms in some patients. This essay examines cognitive and brain-imaging associations reported mainly in frequent, high-dose nonmedical users, and asks how those findings should inform caution. They do not establish that supervised therapeutic treatment causes lasting cognitive decline. Longer-term esketamine studies now provide useful, though incomplete, counterevidence. The proposed link between cognition, social connection, and treatment outcomes remains a hypothesis.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Findings in Frequent Nonmedical Ketamine Users</h2>
        <p>The clearest evidence comes from people who used ketamine repeatedly outside medical settings. In a one-year longitudinal study of 150 people, frequent ketamine users showed impairments in spatial working memory and pattern recognition memory that worsened as their use increased (Morgan et al., 2010). Brain imaging shows structural changes to match. Liao et al. (2011) compared 41 ketamine-dependent people with 44 drug-free controls and found reduced gray matter in the frontal cortex, including the left superior frontal gyrus; the reduction grew with duration of use and cumulative dose. The same group reported abnormalities in frontal white matter (Liao et al., 2010) and, more recently, widespread thinning of the frontal and parietal cortex in people with ketamine use disorder (Tang et al., 2024).</p>
        <p>These studies establish association, not proof of cause. Users may have differed from controls before they began using ketamine, and they took doses far larger and more frequent than a clinic would give. The dose-dependent pattern in Liao et al. (2011), however, is compatible with a causal contribution but does not rule out confounding.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Language, Meaning, and Mood</h2>

        <h3>Verbal and Semantic Functions</h3>
        <p>Ketamine appears to act on language-related processing in a way other amnestic drugs do not. Morgan and Curran (2006) reviewed evidence that ketamine, unlike classic amnestic drugs, may impair semantic memory, the store of word meanings and concepts on which language depends, and that episodic memory impairment in chronic users may not resolve after they stop. Chan et al. (2013) found that chronic users had impaired verbal fluency, processing speed, and verbal learning, and that the verbal learning deficit tracked lifetime ketamine use. The authors concluded that the impairment may be specific to verbal information processing. No study reviewed here shows direct damage to classic language areas such as Broca's or Wernicke's area; the evidence concerns verbal and semantic function and the frontal regions that support it.</p>

        <h3>Depression Over Time</h3>
        <p>The most troubling finding runs against ketamine's reputation. In the longitudinal study, frequent users reported more depressive symptoms, and those symptoms increased significantly over the year; delusional symptoms rose as well (Morgan et al., 2010). A drug that relieves depression in a single dose may, with frequent long-term use, be associated with more of it.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Why the Brain Needs Social Connection</h2>
        <p>Human beings are built for connection, and the evidence for its importance is strong. A meta-analysis of 148 studies found that people with stronger social relationships had a 50% greater odds of survival over the follow-up periods studied (Holt-Lunstad et al., 2010). Connection also shapes mood. Among 3,005 older Americans followed over time, social disconnectedness, such as a small network and infrequent contact, predicted greater perceived isolation, which in turn predicted more depressive and anxiety symptoms (Santini et al., 2020).</p>
        <p>Conversation, shared meaning, and memory of others are built from the very capacities reported as impaired in some frequent nonmedical users: verbal fluency, semantic memory, and episodic memory. This link is an inference, not a tested finding. No study reviewed here measured whether ketamine users lose social connections as a result of these impairments. The hypothesis is nonetheless plausible, testable, and important: if repeated ketamine gradually erodes the tools a person uses to connect, it could undermine one of the strongest protections against depression while appearing to treat it.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>The Problem With a Blanket Treatment</h2>
        <p>An earlier systematic review of ketamine for depression found that only 20% of studies assessed long-term side effects, and the authors identified a selective reporting bias that left the safety of repeated dosing largely unexamined (Short et al., 2018). That 2018 assessment should not be treated as a summary of all subsequent clinical evidence.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Counterevidence</h2><p>Later clinical evidence adds an important distinction. The open-label SUSTAIN-3 interim study followed 1,148 participants receiving supervised esketamine plus an oral antidepressant, with exposure up to 4.5 years. Depression improvement generally persisted among those remaining in treatment, and no new safety signal was identified (Zaki et al., 2023). Selection of continuing patients, attrition, and the absence of a randomized long-term comparator limit causal conclusions. These results neither establish risk-free treatment nor justify transferring findings from heavy nonmedical ketamine use directly to supervised esketamine.</p>
        <p>The opposing case deserves a fair hearing. The harms above come mainly from recreational users taking high, frequent, unsupervised doses. A systematic review of maintenance ketamine treatment in treatment-resistant depression found it effective in sustaining antidepressant response and reported that cognitive impairment, addiction, and urinary problems seemed uncommon (Smith-Apeldoorn et al., 2022). That review, however, rested on only three randomized trials, most of its evidence came from open-label studies and case reports.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Conclusion</h2>
        <p>Ketamine and esketamine may offer benefits for appropriately selected patients. Findings in frequent nonmedical users warrant concern, but cannot establish the long-term risks of supervised treatment. Individual assessment and ongoing monitoring should weigh benefits, adverse effects, cognition, and everyday functioning. Whether treatment-related cognitive changes undermine social connection remains untested in the sources discussed here.</p>
      </section>

